1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sam, the youngest son of William and Martha Smith attended the Kangarilla School and there he learned the trade of a blacksmith.  He carried on the trade when he went to live at Meadows.   He has recorded that the 
2 CONC early sailing vessels coming out to take back the wheat harvest would bring farm implements back as ballast--tools for cultivation and the Howard, Hornsby, Grey, Ransom and Sims ploughs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Poem by Sam
2 CONT
2 CONT Youth is the time of laughter and song,
2 CONT
2 CONT And the carefree life of a country lad,
2 CONT
2 CONT With the open fields in sunshine gay,
2 CONT
2 CONT And the vigour of life that maketh glad.
2 CONT
2 CONT E'en though he worked at the break of day
2 CONT
2 CONT You could not dampen his ardent will,
2 CONT
2 CONT For he was built of the finest type
2 CONT
2 CONT And prided himself in strength and skill.
2 CONT
2 CONT He would swing the scythe with a manly stroke
2 CONT
2 CONT And the smile of youth as he tossed the hay
2 CONT
2 CONT In the open air; with his sun-tanned face
2 CONT
2 CONT It appeared to be only youthful play.
