1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''On Wikipedia:''' https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drengman_Aaker
2 CONT
2 CONT http://politicalstrangenames.blogspot.com/2013/11/drengman-olsen-aaker-1839-1894.html
2 CONT
2 CONT     One of several children born to Ole Drengmansen and Aslaug Gunleiksdatter Moen Aaker, Drengman O. Aaker was born in Hjartdahl, Telemarken, Norway on September 29, 1839 and in 1848 immigrated to the United States with his famil
2 CONC y via the ship "Lolland". A genealogical website denoting the history of the Aaker family notes that the "Lolland" reached New York in August of 1848 and Drengman and his family later settled in Waukesha County, Wisconsin. The fam
2 CONC ily resided here until around 1854, whereafter they removed to Iowa, settling near the town of Burr Oak Springs. 
2 CONT
2 CONT   Despite being a recent transplant to the United States, Drengman Aaker proved that his patriotism ran high, and in 1862 enlisted in Co. G. of the 12th Iowa Volunteer Infantry. He served throughout the Civil War's duration and wa
2 CONC s mustered out of service in 1866. Following his return home Aaker removed to the town of Ridgeway in Winneshiek County and married in 1869 to Christina Andersdatter Turvold (1849-1922). Aaker and his wife later later became the p
2 CONC arents of four children, Lena (1870-1944), John Theodore (1873-1947), Adolph Oscar (born 1878) and Casper Drengman (1883-1960).
